{"key":"apphy2-mcq-003","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"The second law states total entropy of an isolated system never decreases; spontaneous heat flow from hot to cold increases the total entropy. The cold object's entropy actually increases as it gains heat, so D is false.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.6 Entropy and the Second Law of Thermodynamics","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8280,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":88,"cacheReadTokens":8192},"outputTokens":656,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":133,"reasoningTokens":523},"totalTokens":8936,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8280,"completion_tokens":656,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":8192,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":88,"total_tokens":8936,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":8192},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":523}},"reasoningTokens":523,"cachedInputTokens":8192},"ms":6521,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-003","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"According to the Second Law of Thermodynamics, spontaneous thermal energy transfer occurs in the direction that increases the total entropy of an isolated system. As heat transfers from a higher-temperature object to a lower-temperature object, the entropy gained by the cold object exceeds the entropy lost by the hot object.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.6","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8639,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8639,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":521,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":152,"reasoningTokens":369},"totalTokens":9160,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":369,"promptTokenCount":8639,"candidatesTokenCount":152,"totalTokenCount":9160,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8639}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":152}]},"reasoningTokens":369,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":6993,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-006","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"Each positive charge creates an electric field pointing away from itself. At the midpoint, the distances to the charges are equal, so the electric fields have equal magnitudes but point in opposite directions (leftward from the right charge, rightward from the left charge). The vector sum is zero.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8626,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8626,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":283,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":153,"reasoningTokens":130},"totalTokens":8909,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":130,"promptTokenCount":8626,"candidatesTokenCount":153,"totalTokenCount":8909,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8626}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":153}]},"reasoningTokens":130,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":3766,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-009","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"Immediately after connection, an uncharged capacitor has zero charge and therefore zero potential difference across its plates (ΔV = Q/C = 0). As a result, charge flows freely and the capacitor acts approximately like an ideal wire with zero resistance.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"11.8","required_units":[11],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8624,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8624,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":357,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":146,"reasoningTokens":211},"totalTokens":8981,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":211,"promptTokenCount":8624,"candidatesTokenCount":146,"totalTokenCount":8981,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8624}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":146}]},"reasoningTokens":211,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":3892,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-006","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"At the midpoint, the two identical positive charges produce electric fields of equal magnitude. The left charge's field points right, and the right charge's field points left, so they cancel exactly. The net electric field is therefore zero.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8268,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":76,"cacheReadTokens":8192},"outputTokens":527,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":128,"reasoningTokens":399},"totalTokens":8795,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8268,"completion_tokens":527,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":8192,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":76,"total_tokens":8795,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":8192},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":399}},"reasoningTokens":399,"cachedInputTokens":8192},"ms":4997,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-005","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"An electric field exerts a force $\\vec{F} = q\\vec{E}$ on a positive charge in the direction of $\\vec{E}$. Moving the charge opposite $\\vec{E}$ requires external work against the electrostatic force ($W_E < 0$), moving it toward higher electric potential ($\\Delta V > 0$). Since $\\Delta U_E = q\\Delta V$, potential energy increases.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.4","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8611,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":4580,"cacheReadTokens":4031},"outputTokens":776,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":186,"reasoningTokens":590},"totalTokens":9387,"raw":{"cachedContentTokenCount":4031,"thoughtsTokenCount":590,"promptTokenCount":8611,"candidatesTokenCount":186,"totalTokenCount":9387,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8611}],"cacheTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":4031}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":186}]},"reasoningTokens":590,"cachedInputTokens":4031},"ms":7273,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-025","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Electric field relates to the spatial rate of change of electric potential, not the absolute value of potential. For example, midway between two equal and opposite charges (+q and -q), the electric potential is zero by superposition, but the electric field is nonzero and points from positive to negative.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.5","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Medium"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8613,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8613,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":421,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":153,"reasoningTokens":268},"totalTokens":9034,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":268,"promptTokenCount":8613,"candidatesTokenCount":153,"totalTokenCount":9034,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8613}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":153}]},"reasoningTokens":268,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5040,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-025","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Electric potential is scalar and can be set to zero at an arbitrary reference point; it does not determine electric field. For example, midway between equal positive and negative point charges, V = 0 while E is nonzero. In a uniform field, any point can be assigned V = 0 while E remains constant.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.5","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8255,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":63,"cacheReadTokens":8192},"outputTokens":814,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":145,"reasoningTokens":669},"totalTokens":9069,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8255,"completion_tokens":814,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":8192,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":63,"total_tokens":9069,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":8192},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":669}},"reasoningTokens":669,"cachedInputTokens":8192},"ms":8600,"error":""}
